u}$ fixed as labeled, with error bars and labels giving the 1$\sigma$ quantiles. Replacing the CFA by the DFA in ncdm adds $0.02$–$0.06$ to the fractional overhead, i.e. only $1$–$4\%$ in total runtime. For both evolvers, gbh spends $\approx 1.2$ times as long as ncdm with the CFA on the massive neutrino sector, corresponding to $6$–$8\%$ in total runtime. No gbh point is shown at $M_{
u}=1\mathrm{eV}$ for ndf15, as the code switches to rk above $0.5\mathrm{eV}$; see text for more details. Synchronous-gauge results, while not shown, exhibit similar behavior.

Marginalized posteriors from MCMC analyses of Planck PR3 TTTEEE$+\phi\phi$ data, sampling $\Lambda$CDM$+M_{
u}$, computed with the standard CLASS ncdm module (red) and with the GBH$+$DFA implementation (blue). Data, likelihoods, priors, and sampler settings are identical between the two; only the treatment of the massive neutrino perturbations differs. Top row: one-dimensional marginals for the $\Lambda$CDM parameters, with medians and $68\%$ quantiles annotated. Bottom row: joint posteriors of $M_{
u}$ with each parameter, and the $M_{
u}$ marginal itself, with $95\%$ upper limits annotated. The contours indicate the 1 and 2$\sigma$ of the two-dimensional joint posterior density for pairs of parameters (i.e., the 39.3% and 86.5% mass levels). All median values agree to better than $0.05\sigma$ and the contours overlap throughout.
